[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — NaN sign lost through `DotProduct` (found, fixed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Whenever two arguments were NaNs of *different* signs, the sign of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the printed NaNs differed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | argv | C | Rust (before) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `nan -nan 1` | `nan -nan nan` | `nan -nan -nan` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-nan nan 1` | `-nan nan -nan` | `-nan nan nan` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `nan 1 -nan` | `nan nan -nan` | `nan -nan -nan` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1 nan -nan` | `nan nan -nan` | `-nan nan -nan` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Exit status and stderr matched; only stdout differed. Roughly 0.2% of randomly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m generated triples hit this, and every failing case involved at least two NaNs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with opposite signs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** NaN propagation is *not* commutative on x86-64. For the scalar SSE
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instructions `addss`/`subss`/`mulss` the destination operand is the left-hand
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m operand of the C expression, and the rule (verified by compiling a probe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program on this target) is:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * if the left operand is a NaN, the result is that NaN — sign and payload
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   preserved, quieted if it was signaling;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * otherwise, if the right operand is a NaN, the result is that NaN, quieted;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * otherwise, if the operation is invalid (`inf - inf`, `0 * inf`) the result is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the x86 QNaN "floating-point indefinite", `0xFFC0_0000` — note the **set sign
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bit**, so `printf("%f")` renders it as `-nan`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust's `a + b` and `a * b` carry no such operand-order guarantee, and LLVM
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m does in fact commute them: for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `v[0]*v[0] + v[1]*v[1] + v[2]*v[2]` it emitted the additions with the sources
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m swapped relative to gcc. With ordinary values that is invisible, but it changes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *which* NaN survives the sum. The surviving NaN becomes `Q_rsqrt`'s argument,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m its sign is carried unchanged through the whole of `Q_rsqrt`, and it is then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the sign printed for every vector component that is not itself a NaN.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/fops.rs` adds `fadd`, `fsub` and `fmul`, which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m implement the propagation rule above explicitly, and the three call sites now
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m use them with the C expressions' operand order preserved:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `dot_product` — `fadd(fadd(fmul(x0,y0), fmul(x1,y1)), fmul(x2,y2))`, matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the left-associated `DotProduct` macro at `q_shared.h:365`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `q_rsqrt` — `fmul(number, 0.5)` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `fmul(y, fsub(threehalfs, fmul(fmul(x2, y), y)))`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `vector_normalize_fast` — `v[i] = fmul(v[i], ilength)`, since `v[i] *=
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ilength` puts `v[i]` in the destination.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This also pins down the two invalid-operation results that the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m prints as `-nan` (`inf 0 0` → `-inf -nan -nan`), so they no longer depend on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m LLVM choosing not to constant-fold.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by the `mixed_sign_nans_select_the_surviving_nan`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `invalid_operations_produce_negative_nan` and `nans_mixed_with_infinities`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tests.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Checked and found already correct
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were all candidate mismatches that testing cleared, recorded so the next
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reader does not have to re-derive them:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`atof` on unparsable input.** `atof` reports no errors, so `driver abc def
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ghi` prints `0.000000 0.000000 0.000000` and exits 0. The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reimplementation in `src/clib.rs` matches glibc `strtod` bit for bit over
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ~244,000 strings, including hex floats, `0x` with no digits (only the leading
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0` is consumed), truncated `infinity` prefixes, `nan(payload)`, `double`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   subnormal rounding boundaries and 1000-digit mantissas. Verified in both the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `dev` and `release` profiles, so the `u128` shifts in `compose` do not trip
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   debug overflow checks.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`%f` formatting.** Rust's `{:.6}` agrees with glibc's `%f` on all ~240,000
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `float` bit patterns tried, including every exact tie at the sixth decimal
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (odd multiples of `1/128`, where both round half to even) and `-0.000000`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   glibc prints non-finite values as `nan` / `-nan` / `inf` / `-inf` without a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   precision field, which `format_f` special-cases.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`argc == 0`.** Executing the binary with an empty `argv` produces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `" requires 4 inputs\n"` and exit 1 from both programs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Non-UTF-8 arguments.** `main.rs` reads `args_os()` as raw bytes, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   arguments that are not valid UTF-8 are parsed and echoed exactly as C's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `char*` arguments are. (A NUL byte cannot occur inside an `argv` entry, so it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is not a reachable input class.)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`Q_rsqrt`'s bit hack.** `f32::to_bits` / `f32::from_bits` reproduce the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `memcpy` punning, and `0x5f3759dfu32.wrapping_sub(i >> 1)` reproduces the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `uint32_t` subtraction. The subtraction can never yield NaN bits, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   final multiply's destination operand is always a real number.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Unreached C code.** `idppc` is `0` (`q_shared.h:70`), so the `#if !idppc`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   definition of `Q_rsqrt` is the one compiled, and `#if 1` (`q_shared.h:363`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   selects the macro form of `DotProduct`. Nothing else in `q_math.c` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reachable from `main`, which calls only `atof`, `VectorNormalizeFast`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `printf`, `fprintf` and `exit`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
