[1mThe bug: NaN signs were wrong.[22m When two arguments were NaNs of opposite signs, stdout differed:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
| argv | C | Rust (before) |[0m[0m
|---|---|---|[0m[0m
| [38;5;10mnan -nan 1[0m | [38;5;10mnan -nan nan[0m | [38;5;10mnan -nan -nan[0m |[0m[0m
| [38;5;10m1 nan -nan[0m | [38;5;10mnan nan -nan[0m | [38;5;10m-nan nan -nan[0m |[0m[0m
[0m[0m
NaN propagation is not commutative on x86-64: for [38;5;10maddss[0m/[38;5;10mmulss[0m, if the left (destination) operand is a NaN it wins, otherwise the right one does. Rust's [38;5;10ma + b[0m gives no operand-order guarantee, and LLVM emitted [38;5;10mDotProduct[0m's additions with the sources swapped relative to gcc — so a different NaN survived the sum, became [38;5;10mQ_rsqrt[0m's argument, and its sign was then printed for every component that wasn't itself a NaN.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
